  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Starting a Signal with a Unit Step",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a zero-before-start continuous-time signal from piecewise form into a step-gated expression by multiplying the active formula by the unit step that turns on at the start time.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Starting a Signal with a Unit Step",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Starting a Signal with a Unit Step",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When a continuous-time signal is zero before a start time and follows one active formula after that time, the compact form is the active formula multiplied by a unit step. The unit step is the switch that marks where the signal turns on.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Think of the left side of the switch as the inactive interval and the right side as the active interval. In this lesson, the inactive side is $t<t_0$, the active side is $t\\ge t_0$, and the step is what separates the two.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the simplest case, place the switch at $t=0$. Then a signal that is $0$ for $t<0$ and $f(t)$ for $t\\ge 0$ becomes a single gated expression.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=f(t)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For any later start time $t_0$, keep the active formula unchanged and shift only the step to $u(t-t_0)$. That shift moves the turn-on to the exact start time without changing the signal shape after the switch.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=f(t)u(t-t_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the lesson's boundary convention consistently at the switch instant, and remember the key rule: the step contributes the timing, while the active formula contributes the shape. That is the conversion you will reuse in the next steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Starting a Signal with a Unit Step",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Starting an Active Formula at $t = 0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write a unit-step form for $x(t)=0$ for $t<0$ and $x(t)=2t+1$ for $t\\ge 0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a two-case signal with zero before the start and one active formula after the start. Because the switch is at the origin, the gate is $u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the active branch exactly as $2t+1$. The step provides the on-off timing; it does not change the formula after $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=(2t+1)u(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check the inactive side: for $t<0$, $u(t)=0$, so the whole expression is zero before the start. That matches the original piecewise definition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Starting a Signal with a Unit Step",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Starting an Active Formula After $t = 0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $x(t)=0$ for $t<2$ and $x(t)=2e^{-t}$ for $t\\ge 2$ to a unit-step expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a signal that is zero before $t=2$ and follows one active formula after that time. Because the switch happens at $t=2$, the gate is $u(t-2)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the active branch exactly as $2e^{-t}$. Only the step argument shifts; the formula itself stays unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2e^{-t}u(t-2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check both sides: for $t<2$, $u(t-2)=0$, so the expression is zero; for $t\\ge 2$, $u(t-2)=1$, so the output is $2e^{-t}$. That matches the piecewise definition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Starting a Signal with a Unit Step",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Starting an Active Formula Before $t = 0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $x(t)=0$ for $t<-1$ and $x(t)=\\sin(t)$ for $t\\ge -1$ into a unit-step expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a signal that is zero before $t=-1$ and active from $t=-1$ onward. Since the switch turns on one unit before the origin, the gate is $u(t+1)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the active branch exactly as $\\sin(t)$. Only the step argument changes to reflect the negative start time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\sin(t)u(t+1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Check both intervals: for $t<-1$, $u(t+1)=0$, so the signal is zero; for $t\\ge -1$, $u(t+1)=1$, so the output is $\\sin(t)$. That preserves the piecewise definition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
